# How a fridge keeps cold
subtitle: "Follow the coolant as it boils inside, is squeezed, and dumps your food's heat out the back: a fridge moves heat, it makes no cold."

## How a fridge keeps cold
    shape cabinet shape rect x 300 y 410 width 400 height 460 hidden true
    text l_inside "inside: 4 °C" #2f6b8f x 300 y 610 font_size 30 hidden true
    shape milk shape rect x 200 y 240 width 70 height 120 hidden true
    text l_milk "milk" x 272 y 240 font_size 30 hidden true
    path evap #2f6b8f details "This part is the evaporator. Most home fridges today use isobutane (R600a) as the coolant; at the low pressure inside this pipe it boils far below freezing." points [[500,545],[140,545],[140,505],[460,505]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    text l_evap "boils at −20 °C" #2f6b8f x 312 y 468 font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow heat_in thick #d0865f from.anchor top from.ref milk to.x 200 to.y 493 hidden true
    text l_heat_in "heat" #d0865f x 238 y 398 font_size 30 hidden true
    path p1 #2f6b8f points [[460,505],[475,505],[475,220],[565,220]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    shape compressor #8a5fa8 details "The compressor is an electric pump. Squeezing a gas does work on it, and that work shows up as a higher temperature — the same reason a bicycle pump gets warm." shape circle x 610 y 220 radius 45 style.fill #8a5fa8 hidden true
    label l_comp "compressor" right #8a5fa8 attach_to compressor hidden true
    path condenser #b5482e points [[580,325],[580,585],[630,585],[630,325],[680,325],[680,585]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    arrow p2 #b5482e from.anchor top from.ref compressor to.anchor start to.ref condenser style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    label l_cond "back coils, 45 °C" right #b5482e attach_to condenser hidden true
    text kitchen "kitchen: 20 °C" x 880 y 300 font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow leak1 thick #d0865f from.x 20 from.y 420 to.x 125 to.y 420 hidden true
    arrow leak2 thick #d0865f from.x 20 from.y 320 to.x 125 to.y 320 hidden true
    text l_leak "leaks in" #d0865f x 75 y 480 font_size 26 hidden true
    arrow heat_out thick #d0865f from.x 700 from.y 370 to.x 790 to.y 370 hidden true
    label l_heat_out "heat" above #d0865f attach_to heat_out hidden true
    path p3 #b5482e points [[680,585],[680,615],[602,615]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    polygon valve #3d3833 points [[550,631],[575,618],[600,631],[600,599],[575,612],[550,599]] style.fill #8f887c hidden true
    label l_valve "narrow valve" above attach_to valve hidden true
    path p4 #2f6b8f points [[548,615],[525,615],[525,545],[500,545]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    group low_side members ["p4","evap","p1"] hidden true
    group high_side members ["p2","condenser","p3"] hidden true
    text t_gas "gas" #2f6b8f x 440 y 330 font_size 28 hidden true
    text t_liquid "liquid" #b5482e x 700 y 650 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "How a fridge keeps cold"

How does a fridge stay cold in a warm kitchen? The surprise: it makes no cold at all — it carries heat out.
    draw cabinet l_inside milk l_milk

Inside it is 4 degrees, out here 20, and heat always creeps from warm to cold — so warmth leaks in through the walls all day.
    draw kitchen leak1 leak2 l_leak

    erase leak1 leak2 l_leak

So a pipe runs round the inside, carrying a coolant that boils at about minus 20 degrees.
    draw evap l_evap

Colder than the milk, it soaks up the milk's heat, and that heat makes it boil into gas.
    draw heat_in l_heat_in

Boiling swallows a lot of heat — the way drying sweat cools your skin. This is where the inside gets cold.
    highlight evap

    pause 0.4

Now the heat is in the gas. A pump, the compressor, sucks that gas out of the cabinet and squeezes it hard.
    draw p1 compressor l_comp t_gas

    highlight compressor effect pulse color #8a5fa8

Squeezed gas heats up: to about 45 degrees, hotter than the kitchen.
    draw p2 condenser l_cond

So in the coils on the back, heat flows out into the room, and the gas cools back into a liquid.
    draw heat_out l_heat_out t_liquid

    pause 0.3

Last, the liquid squeezes through a narrow valve back into the cabinet. Its pressure drops, and at low pressure it boils at minus 20 again.
    draw p3 valve l_valve p4

Round and round it goes: pick heat up inside, carry it out, dump it behind.
    flow p4 evap p1 p2 condenser p3

The whole trick is pressure: low where the coolant must be colder than the food,
    highlight low_side delivery grave

high where it must be hotter than the room.
    highlight high_side delivery grave

That squeeze is what the electricity pays for: pushing heat uphill, from cold to warm.
    highlight compressor

And it is why the back of a fridge feels warm: that is the heat that was in your food.
    point at.ref heat_out gesture circle

    quiz "On a hot day you leave the fridge door open. What happens to the kitchen?" intro "A quick check."
        right: It gets warmer: the fridge only moves heat from its inside to its back, both in the same room — and its motor adds heat of its own.
        wrong: Think about where the heat taken from inside ends up.
        * It cools down
        + It gets warmer
---
